[9581fc2be3c2f18a] (mini.criticism.v1)
All three proposals in this cycle executed as expected (as_expected: true for each). No expectations failed, meaning no divergence between rule readings and code behavior was exposed by these tests.

Property 1 (recovery - fence priority): STANDS. The claim that fenced JSON objects win over top-level objects regardless of position was confirmed. The code iterates fenced pools before top-level pools: `for pool in (fenced, top_level):` [64db54f68a8d5da6], meaning any fenced object beats any top-level object. The documented rule says 'the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object' [4c0fe8e4f113cf03], which the code implements correctly - fence priority is absolute, not position-dependent across pools.

Property 2 (recovery - duplicate key handling): STANDS. The claim that _loads_last_wins returns None when JSON has no duplicate keys was confirmed. The function explicitly checks `if not seen_duplicates: return None` [64db54f68a8d5da6], meaning it only activates for duplicate-key cases. This matches the docstring stating it handles 'JSON that strict parsing refused only because of repeated keys' [64db54f68a8d5da6].

Property 3 (grounding - case-insensitive value-in-span): STANDS. The claim that value-in-span uses case-insensitive matching was confirmed. The code uses `.casefold()` in the value-in-span check: `if _normalise_whitespace(fields["value"]).casefold() not in _normalise_whitespace(fields["span"]).casefold()` [64db54f68a8d5da6]. This IS a relaxation not documented in the rule, which states 'no relaxation, value-in-span checked' [4c0fe8e4f113cf03]. The property stands as a documented divergence - the code does something the rules don't mention.

What a proposer should try next: Since all three properties were confirmed, proposers should test edge cases around these confirmed behaviors - for example, testing whether the fence regex handles edge cases (nested fences, malformed fences), whether _loads_last_wins handles nested duplicate keys correctly, or whether the casefold() behavior interacts unexpectedly with unicode normalization.

